Logic and philosophy of logic (@logic-and-philosophy-of-logic: 0, is #chapter, #outline) { r_1J0J8EJ093GRB91Y0Z9353TJMR
Logic investigates valid inference and the forms through which claims can support or conflict with one another. This material covers propositions, quantification, consequence, consistency, proof, models, modality, and alternative logical systems, alongside questions about truth, paradox, ordinary reasoning, and the limits of formalisation. Share the relevant formal foundations with [ERG-012](../../../ergon/hephaestus/mathematics/mathematical-foundations-logic-and-proof/Mathematical-foundations-logic-and-proof.lingua#r_1K7J2A99Z4798HQEQ7Z0CKWAE7), introducing notation at the point of use. A philosophical argument, an empirical inference, and a formal derivation should each make their standards explicit.

**Related plans:** [Mathematical foundations, logic, and proof](../../../ergon/hephaestus/mathematics/mathematical-foundations-logic-and-proof/Mathematical-foundations-logic-and-proof.lingua#r_1K7J2A99Z4798HQEQ7Z0CKWAE7).

